# Node dependencies, any depth. No .gitignore at repo root existed before
# this, so nothing stopped a `git add -A` from picking up node_modules in a
# sibling top-level dir (e.g. marketing-site/) that isn't under dataops-agent/,
# whose own .gitignore only covers itself and below.
node_modules/

# Database dumps. These must never enter version control: they are multi-MB,
# they hold real tenant data, and at least one of them
# (dataops_backup_pre_encryption_20260830.sql) predates Item #1's credential
# encryption migration, so it contains data-source passwords as PLAINTEXT JSON
# — committing it would put those in git history permanently, where removing
# them later means a history rewrite rather than a delete.
#
# Until now this directory was merely untracked, not ignored, so a single
# `git add -A` would have swept all three dumps in. Keep the backups themselves
# somewhere durable outside git (external drive / cloud storage).
backups/

# Next.js build output for the marketing site. Deliberately narrow: only the
# build artefacts, NOT marketing-site/ itself — that directory is a separate
# product tracked on its own branch (origin/marketing-site, see CLAUDE.md), and
# a .gitignore entry applies on whatever branch you're standing on, so ignoring
# the whole directory here would hide it on the branch where it belongs.
# .next/ is regenerated output that nobody wants committed on any branch;
# `git add -A` on master currently stages 216 files (~6.4MB) of it.
marketing-site/.next/

# Claude Code's per-machine settings — local by definition (it's in the name),
# not shared configuration. Untracked but unignored until now, so a broad add
# would have swept it in.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Next.js build output, any depth — covers dataops-agent/frontend/.next/ (the
# actual app) the way marketing-site/.next/ above covers that sibling
# product. No leading slash, so it also matches at any nesting depth; kept
# alongside the narrower marketing-site/.next/ entry rather than replacing
# it, since that entry's comment documents a real constraint (don't ignore
# marketing-site/ itself) that's still true.
.next/
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Secrets hygiene scan (2026-10-03, docs/context/GOTCHAS.md): dataops-agent/
# and frontend/ each ignore their own .env already, but nothing at the repo
# root caught a .env created outside those two directories, or a
# non-.local environment-suffixed file (.env.production, .env.staging)
# anywhere -- confirmed both gaps directly with git check-ignore against
# throwaway probe files before adding this. The .example templates must
# stay tracked, hence the negated re-includes below.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Private keys / certificates, any depth -- frontend/.gitignore already had
# *.pem, backend had neither; nothing at root covered either one.
*.pem
*.key
